<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palma</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Palma de Mallorca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palma is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on a wide bay on the island's south-west coast. Founded as a Roman town after 123 BCE by Quintus Caecilius Metellus, it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madina-mayurqa">Madina Mayurqa</a> under Muslim rule and the capital of the Kingdom of Majorca after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> captured it in 1229. Its Gothic cathedral, La Seu, the Almudaina palace and Bellver Castle date from that era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palma.jpg" alt="Palma" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathedrale_von_Palma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1899 the soldier and writer José <b>Palma</b> wrote the Spanish poem &quot;Filipinas&quot;, which was itself drawn from a Kapampangan poem, &quot;Labuad Mapalad&quot; (&quot;Blessed Land&quot;), written in September 1898 by Mariano Proceso Pabalan of Bacolor, Pampanga.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>The outcome of his quest and his fate are unknown; he is memorialized in his native city of <b>Palma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Urged by the Ostrogothic king Theodoric to settle the matter, the bishops met at <b>Palma</b> and declared they could not judge Pope Symmachus, successor of Peter the Apostle, leaving it to God.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Accused of sheltering pirates, the islands were conquered in 123 BCE by the Roman general Quintus Caecilius Metellus, who took the name Balearicus and founded the towns of <b>Palma</b> and Pollentia.</blockquote><a href="/state/balearic-islanders">Balearic Islanders</a></li><li><blockquote>The islands lived from farming, fishing and trade, with towns such as <b>Palma</b>, Pollentia and on Menorca Mago and Iamo.</blockquote><a href="/state/balearica">Balearica</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was a Mediterranean trading state, with <b>Palma</b> and Perpignan as royal seats, merchant fleets reaching North Africa and a famed school of cartographers who made the Catalan Atlas.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
